  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry with anomalous warmth continuing into mid-week. \nDaily high  in the upper 60s to low 70s, with isolated readings into \nthe  mid-70s flirting with daily records.\n\nA persistence forecast for southern New England over the next \nseveral days a the east coast mid level ridge remains overhead, the \nridge axis not passing until late Wednesday. Beneath that ridge \nwe'll continue to enjoy an anomalously warm airmass with 925 mb \ntemperatures sitting in the 11-14C range through the end of the \nweek. Combined with an unusually extended period of dry weather and \ndry antecedent conditions we'll continue to see afternoon high \ntemperatures overachieve most guidance sources, reaching well into \nthe 70s through at least Thursday. Persistent S/SW flow will also \npush dewpoints up through the week leading to a warmer floor for low \ntemperatures, only dropping into the 50s by Thursday morning, closer \nto 60 by Friday morning. This pattern will put close to record \nwarmth territory for some, and regardless a stretch of low to mid \n70s in November is not the norm. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather with continued warmer \ntemperatures expected later this week heading into the weekend, \nwhich would feature periodic chances for rain.\n\nA thermal ridge is expected to continue building over southern New \nEngland towards the midweek timeframe, which will be supportive of \nabove-normal temperatures continuing for Wednesday and Thursday this \nweek. 925 mb temperatures between 12-16C along with SW to S flow \nwill bring the possibility of highs in the upper 60s into the \nlow/mid 70s across southern New England Wednesday and Thursday. \nEnsembles have quite high probabilities of highs exceeding 70F \nWednesday afternoon, and these remain somewhat elevated (up to 90% \nin southern parts of the coastal plain) for Thursday afternoon as \nwell. Guidance doesn't indicate much with regards to a return to \nmore mild temperatures until the second half of the weekend as the \nridging shifts off to the east, but that could vary depending on how \nthe approaching system evolves and tracks across the region.\n\nEnsembles have continued to show a consensus with regards to the \nreturn of more unsettled weather for the second half of this week. A \nmid to upper level trough is expected to move in from the west, \nwhich will help advect in an anomalous moisture plume around 2 to 3 \nstandard deviations above normal over southern New England. Closer \nto the surface, a frontal system associated with a previously more \ndeveloped low pressure is expected to track across the region, \nbringing good chances for wetting rains towards the end of the week. \nExact timing is still a bit uncertain, but latest guidance is \nleaning towards onset being Thursday evening at the earliest in \nwestern MA and CT, then tracking eastward through Friday. Showers \nmay even linger into Sunday for parts of the region. Ensemble \nprobabilities with regards to totals have not changed much from the \novernight forecast, with probabilities for 24-hr totals >= 0.5\" \n(period ending Saturday morning) generally between 30-70% across \nensemble suites. Notably, though, the Canadian and ECMWF ensembles \ndistribute these probabilities across the majority of southern New \nEngland as the front tracks from west to east, while the GEFS has a \nmore focused footprint of higher probabilities and totals over Cape \nCod and some of the coastal plain in SE MA. Given the spread in \nguidance, there is still decent uncertainty regarding the \nprogression and development of the surface features that would \nreally enhance rainfall totals and even introduce the chance for \nsome rumbles of thunder.",
